Review oriented field identities before applying physical laws (@math-fields-review: 0, is #chapter) { r_00835FB8D774B1D9A349D6CC42
This block supplies the common language needed to read field and continuum balances: scalar region accumulation, path circulation, oriented area, normal flux, coordinate measure, divergence/curl, integral identities and localization. Use [Investigate oriented boundaries coordinates and local conservation](Investigate-oriented-boundaries-coordinates-and-local-conservation.lingua#r_008752CDDEC92582D0542CEC80) as the connected evidence, and return to [Distinguish a state differential from a path dependent account](Distinguish-a-state-differential-from-a-path-dependent-account.lingua#r_00204337E177A0330276621E37) for the narrower potential identity. A general field need not have a potential; a local derivative identity does not erase holes or singularities.

Explain the chain in your own notation: a derivative matrix approximates a coordinate change; determinants measure signed local volume; absolute determinants transform scalar measure; cross products orient surface elements; the fundamental theorem proves rectangle/box identities; the chain rule and mixed-partial cancellation prove the patch Stokes identity. Name the accepted general integration and curved-domain theorems separately. Do not describe a finite computation as their proof.

Before applying a theorem, identify its domain, regularity, field components, metric, orientation and complete boundary. Recover the polynomial box total 96 and graph circulation −8 by independent boundary and region calculations. Exhibit a shared-boundary cancellation, a deliberately inconsistent sign and the punctured-domain counterexample. Explain why a scalar surface total and flux can differ on the same patch. Establish the exact quadrature class before using the program on a changed example.

For a balance, identify ρ,J,s and the region's motion status. State the extra premise that the integral law holds on every small region, and prove how continuity gives the pointwise equation. Show why one global total and why all integrals of a discontinuous point-supported residual do not give the same conclusion. Separate a balance identity, an empirically supplied transport law, and initial/boundary conditions. None of these supplies the other automatically.

The source notes distinguish inspected passages and one reconstructed calculation from broader reading and future independent work. [Gradient, divergence, curl and integral theorems](../../physics/electromagnetism/mathematics/Gradient-divergence-curl-and-integral-theorems.lingua#r_05M47N16QDCC5B927TEYXAMMA4) and [Integral and local conservation of mass](../../physics/fluid-dynamics/balances/Integral-and-local-conservation-of-mass.lingua#r_788QM37A7NFT52RQGTMS01Q89Q) can now be read with their common calculus preparation explicit. Magnetic and induction foundations are the next physics work; spacetime/gravity and astronomical evidence remain later common gaps. General PDEs, differential geometry, distribution theory, advanced numerical integration and the full calculus/analysis subjects remain branch formation. Mathematics now has 111 common learning Records; this is a coverage checkpoint, not completion of the whole core or any pending subject.

**Return:** [Mathematics](../Mathematics.lingua#r_0BDE857C410434BA79D017008C).
